10
00:00:47,150 --> 00:00:49,550
Luke, is there anything I missed that you would like to add to your introduction?

11
00:00:49,560 --> 00:00:54,230
No, you pretty much covered it all there. Yeah, and in a very succinct and on point.

12
00:00:54,240 --> 00:00:56,590
Thanks.

13
00:00:56,590 --> 00:01:00,270
Luke, if you can start off by explaining the increased role of AI coding agents in improving program.

14
00:01:00,280 --> 00:01:05,390
productivity. Certainly.

15
00:01:05,390 --> 00:01:12,230
So, this really harks back to 2017 when the paper came out of Google around the Transformers architecture.

16
00:01:12,230 --> 00:01:15,750
The paper was called Attention is all that you need.

17
00:01:15,750 --> 00:01:17,990
It's speaking about the attention mechanism.

18
00:01:17,990 --> 00:01:21,670
Prior to then, we'd had AI machine learning have been around for quite some time,

19
00:01:21,670 --> 00:01:27,110
but we hadn't really seen any killer applications of it beyond statistical analysis.

20
00:01:27,110 --> 00:01:30,550
and people would use it as a business tool essentially.

21
00:01:30,550 --> 00:01:34,230
And so, we started to see these sort of general-purpose models come out.

22
00:01:34,230 --> 00:01:36,710
And then quite early on, there was of course GitHub Copilot.

23
00:01:36,710 --> 00:01:42,670
And this is where it was quite astonishing really.

24
00:01:42,670 --> 00:01:44,350
Even though the quality was not quite there,

25
00:01:44,350 --> 00:01:49,830
the ability for these machines to generate code, it was noticed.

26
00:01:49,830 --> 00:01:54,991
as being a very clear emerging use case application of these models was how good they were at. generating code.

27
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:59,550
And initially, we started off with a system called fill in the middle.

28
00:01:59,550 --> 00:02:04,550
And fill in the middle is auto completion of code.

29
00:02:04,550 --> 00:02:10,870
And what happens is the code is generated based on the prepend and the append.

30
00:02:10,870 --> 00:02:17,150
So, the content that surrounds the prompt, or effectively where the cursor is situated within a IDE.

31
00:02:17,150 --> 00:02:22,910
Okay, so the prompt would fill up the code that's above, say 10 lines,

32
00:02:22,910 --> 00:02:25,550
and the 10 lines below the cursor.

33
00:02:25,550 --> 00:02:29,350
And then the model would have to predict what is in the middle.

34
00:02:29,350 --> 00:02:31,870
So, it was called fill in the middle.

35
00:02:31,870 --> 00:02:33,510
FIM is the term that they use.

36
00:02:33,510 --> 00:02:36,110
So, I think this was one of the first times.

37
00:02:36,110 --> 00:02:40,630
that we started to see AI really start to get some traction with software engineers.

38
00:02:40,630 --> 00:02:45,470
And you got this auto complete magical to complete functionality that came around.

39
00:02:45,470 --> 00:02:51,310
And that was really where there would have definitely been a marked increase in productivity for people.

40
00:02:51,310 --> 00:02:53,190
that were using that.

41
00:02:53,190 --> 00:02:57,270
because as a software engineer, there would be a lot of tasks that you would perform.

42
00:02:57,270 --> 00:03:02,590
where you wouldn't really need to exercise any level of really pushing your brain to the limits.

43
00:03:02,590 --> 00:03:07,470
There would be things such as hashing out structs and setting up functions,

44
00:03:07,470 --> 00:03:12,190
and just sort of general housekeeping type tasks.

45
00:03:12,190 --> 00:03:14,030
And AI proved to be very good at doing that.

46
00:03:14,030 --> 00:03:16,509
I remember when I started to use Copilot myself,

47
00:03:16,509 --> 00:03:21,670
I was quite surprised at how it would understand precisely the response format.

48
00:03:21,670 --> 00:03:24,030
that I would need to set out.

49
00:03:24,030 --> 00:03:28,910
for a struct or it would somehow magically understand what I needed for a JSON structure.

50
00:03:28,910 --> 00:03:32,750
And this is where we really started to see, I think, a productivity increase was.

51
00:03:32,750 --> 00:03:37,470
In a lot ways, it was a tool as a developer tool,

52
00:03:37,470 --> 00:03:41,590
in much the same way as when shells started to become more purposed to developers.

53
00:03:41,590 --> 00:03:45,390
and you started to get that sort of rich experience within a terminal.

54
00:03:45,390 --> 00:03:49,750
And people made them very much their own thing because it improved their productivity essentially.

55
00:03:49,750 --> 00:03:56,670
And that was the start of the productivity gain that has happened within software development.

56
00:03:56,670 --> 00:03:59,870
I think there is a lot more future applications of AI.

57
00:03:59,870 --> 00:04:04,750
that will increase productivity and will help a lot to reduce churn.

58
00:04:04,750 --> 00:04:08,870
It will take away the grind of a lot of stuff that engineers have had to do,

59
00:04:08,870 --> 00:04:10,550
they've had to live with and accept.

60
00:04:10,550 --> 00:04:14,670
I think we'll start to see AI factor those way over time as well.

61
00:04:14,680 --> 00:04:16,949
All right.

62
00:04:16,949 --> 00:04:19,630
You've covered GitHub Copilot in one of our previous episodes, 533.

63
00:04:19,640 --> 00:04:21,910
I'll make sure we'll link it to that.

64
00:04:21,920 --> 00:04:27,710
Have any studies been done to quantify the improved productivity by using AI coding assistance?

65
00:04:27,720 --> 00:04:30,430
Yes, there are.

66
00:04:30,430 --> 00:04:34,590
Now, people have different views as to how accurate they are,

67
00:04:34,590 --> 00:04:37,190
but one of the ones that I heard was it was related to GitHub.

68
00:04:37,190 --> 00:04:40,550
and it was around the region of 45 to 50%.

69
00:04:40,550 --> 00:04:42,390
I cannot remember the exact figure now,

70
00:04:42,390 --> 00:04:49,110
but there's quite a substantial claim that productivity had been significantly raised through the use of AI.

71
00:04:49,120 --> 00:04:50,870
That's quite a large number.

72
00:04:50,880 --> 00:04:52,870
Certainly it certainly is.

73
00:04:52,870 --> 00:04:57,670
And we have noticed a marked ability to be able to move more quickly with what we build,

74
00:04:57,680 --> 00:05:00,230
thank you to AI.

75
00:05:00,230 --> 00:05:06,630
So, I'm part of a startup and we use AI very effectively for prototyping.

76
00:05:06,630 --> 00:05:09,310
Okay, so we wouldn't really trust it in a large code base.

77
00:05:09,310 --> 00:05:13,830
It just doesn't handle large code bases so well,

78
00:05:13,830 --> 00:05:17,830
but when you very quickly want to prototype and build something, that's where AI is exceptionally strong.

79
00:05:17,830 --> 00:05:23,270
To move from zero to something you can act very quickly.

80
00:05:23,270 --> 00:05:26,710
and that's very useful in a fast-moving environment such as a startup.

81
00:05:26,710 --> 00:05:32,510
where you need to quickly validate ideas, you need to connect up and integrate different systems,

82
00:05:32,510 --> 00:05:35,590
and AI can be great there cuz you just want to see something wiggle.

83
00:05:35,590 --> 00:05:37,430
You want to see something working.

84
00:05:37,430 --> 00:05:40,230
You want to prove the hypothesis to yourself.

85
00:05:40,230 --> 00:05:45,830
And that's where I found I I've really embraced AI myself is to do a lot of tasks.

86
00:05:45,830 --> 00:05:47,990
where I need to move fast, I need to be reactive,

87
00:05:47,990 --> 00:05:52,190
I want to try something out, I want to validate something.

88
00:05:52,190 --> 00:05:54,070
That is where AI's really come into its own.

89
00:05:54,070 --> 00:05:58,390
For myself and some of the engineers that I have in my company working on producing software.

90
00:05:58,400 --> 00:06:03,830
And what are the key risks introduced due to the reliance on AI coding assistance?

91
00:06:03,840 --> 00:06:06,910
So, there are many.

92
00:06:06,910 --> 00:06:10,510
Okay, some of them are old risks within the new world.

93
00:06:10,510 --> 00:06:17,150
We looked at coding assistance and did some studies on their security.

94
00:06:17,150 --> 00:06:20,750
What are the risks?

95
00:06:20,750 --> 00:06:26,190
And I think one of the clear ones that would became immediately present was data exfiltration,

96
00:06:26,190 --> 00:06:29,270
even if unwillingly.

97
00:06:29,270 --> 00:06:33,030
The leakage of secrets, tokens, passwords, personal information.

98
00:06:33,040 --> 00:06:37,790
Essentially, coding assistants, they rely on context.

99
00:06:37,790 --> 00:06:40,790
Context is really important.

100
00:06:40,790 --> 00:06:42,790
So, context is essentially snippets of code.

101
00:06:42,790 --> 00:06:50,390
They need to understand the entire or at least a section of a syntax tree within code,

102
00:06:50,390 --> 00:06:55,350
so that they can make changes without breaking other functions or methods.

103
00:06:55,350 --> 00:06:57,190
that belong to a particular class that they're working within.

104
00:06:57,190 --> 00:07:00,909
So, they like to grab entire files. That's one of the things you'll see quite often.

105
00:07:00,919 --> 00:07:04,710
when you're working with a coding assistant such as Cursor.

106
00:07:04,710 --> 00:07:10,390
or Copilot, they will take a whole file via the IDE, they'll load it into a prompt,

107
00:07:10,390 --> 00:07:15,030
and then that prompt will then be sent to an endpoint.

108
00:07:15,030 --> 00:07:17,670
where it'll be canonicalized and serialized and put into a format.

109
00:07:17,680 --> 00:07:23,030
that the LLM can then make a prediction around what the task is at hand.

110
00:07:23,030 --> 00:07:25,710
It'll start to generate code.

111
00:07:25,710 --> 00:07:31,030
And quite often, these files can be it could be cryptographic keys,

112
00:07:31,030 --> 00:07:39,071
they could be.env files where people have database passwords and perhaps for a production site. It could be API tokens.

113
00:07:39,071 --> 00:07:43,550
Quite often, I spoke about earlier being able to prototype very quickly,

114
00:07:43,550 --> 00:07:45,790
that's one of the useful things for AI.

115
00:07:45,790 --> 00:07:47,550
We've all done it as software engineers.

116
00:07:47,550 --> 00:07:50,710
You are writing a single script.

117
00:07:50,710 --> 00:07:53,310
You want to see an API work.

118
00:07:53,310 --> 00:07:56,470
You hardcode the token in because you know you're not going to push it to GitHub.

119
00:07:56,470 --> 00:07:59,670
You're certainly not going to deploy it, but you just want to see the thing work.

120
00:07:59,670 --> 00:08:01,390
So, you take a token and you hardcode it.

121
00:08:01,390 --> 00:08:06,910
And those effectively are captured by the IDE and they're all sent out.

122
00:08:06,910 --> 00:08:11,830
And these keys are I mean, it's you could discuss the receiving party could be trusted.

123
00:08:11,830 --> 00:08:20,070
Could be a large corporation, Microsoft Azure or Anthropic or wherever the cloud service is running.

124
00:08:20,070 --> 00:08:24,550
They may well be entities that you should never leak keys,

125
00:08:24,550 --> 00:08:26,909
but they're not likely to exploit you by receiving those keys.

126
00:08:26,909 --> 00:08:28,870
But I think the other concern is.

127
00:08:28,870 --> 00:08:34,350
that quite often the data that is received is used to train models.

128
00:08:34,350 --> 00:08:36,510
So, hypothetically, it's not too far-fetched to have a scenario.

129
00:08:36,520 --> 00:08:43,589
where a large language model may spit out a token that it's learned during its training phase.

130
00:08:43,589 --> 00:08:45,790
It could spit out a password.

131
00:08:45,790 --> 00:08:48,030
It could spit out a bit of personal information.

132
00:08:48,030 --> 00:08:53,790
You may have a spreadsheet that you by accident put within a folder.

133
00:08:53,790 --> 00:08:55,829
that contains code and that could be picked up by the IDE.

134
00:08:55,829 --> 00:09:03,510
and it could have payslip information, social security numbers, medical information.

135
00:09:03,510 --> 00:09:05,430
And this data can then be used for reinforcement learning.

136
00:09:05,430 --> 00:09:09,430
It can be used to train the model because data is king effectively.

137
00:09:09,430 --> 00:09:13,110
Data is the key value that is derived from a lot of these services.

138
00:09:13,110 --> 00:09:18,270
So, those are the key risks I think for developers is that it's very easy to leak information.

139
00:09:18,270 --> 00:09:26,231
For the actual models themselves, I think some of the other risks are quite often they have a. distant training dates.

140
00:09:26,240 --> 00:09:29,310
Okay, so knowledge is a model is tuned.

141
00:09:29,320 --> 00:09:33,829
and the knowledge is recent at the point of where that training happens.

142
00:09:33,839 --> 00:09:40,230
Right. Now, afterwards, knowledge becomes stale effectively.

143
00:09:40,230 --> 00:09:42,271
So, it's at a certain point in time. It's a snapshot in time.

144
00:09:42,271 --> 00:09:46,390
And because these models are very expensive to do full training,

145
00:09:46,390 --> 00:09:48,350
it's not something that happens every week or month, even.

146
00:09:48,360 --> 00:09:52,550
Sometimes it can be months that will go by.

147
00:09:52,550 --> 00:09:57,030
A model will be in circulation, and then the next new frontier model will come out.

148
00:09:57,030 --> 00:10:00,111
And during that time, security risks can happen. Okay?

149
00:10:00,111 --> 00:10:08,790
Packages can become malicious, maintainer accounts can be overtaken, vulnerabilities can be discovered, critical vulnerabilities.

150
00:10:08,800 --> 00:10:14,230
And the large language model will have no implicit knowledge of that because of its training cutoff date.

151
00:10:14,230 --> 00:10:19,150
So, there is a chance that a model could recommend dangerous content, effectively.

152
00:10:19,150 --> 00:10:22,910
You know, it could introduce security risks into your code.

153
00:10:22,910 --> 00:10:26,350
because it doesn't have that current knowledge that it is a risk.

154
00:10:26,350 --> 00:10:28,711
I guess one way of highlighting this is we remember Log4j. Okay?

155
00:10:28,711 --> 00:10:32,710
This is sort of way before the current batch of large language models.

156
00:10:32,710 --> 00:10:38,150
and the transformer architecture was in public use.

157
00:10:38,150 --> 00:10:40,829
But you imagine a model had been trained, say a week before Log4j,

158
00:10:40,829 --> 00:10:44,990
and then the next new model would not come out for 4 or 5 months later.

159
00:10:44,990 --> 00:10:51,910
It could quite easily be creating people's dependency files for when they want to create a Java project,

160
00:10:51,910 --> 00:10:54,910
and then in there is Log4j, effectively.

161
00:10:54,910 --> 00:10:59,270
So, yeah, I'd say stale knowledge cutoffs are one of the things.

162
00:10:59,270 --> 00:11:01,550
that we look to do in Stack Clock was that we use an embeddings model.

163
00:11:01,550 --> 00:11:10,710
and a retrieval-augmented generation system to bring in the latest threat intelligence around what's dangerous, malicious, archived, deprecated,

164
00:11:10,710 --> 00:11:12,550
and so forth.

165
00:11:12,550 --> 00:11:16,670
So, there are ways that you can get more fresh, recent information in there.

166
00:11:16,670 --> 00:11:18,270
The other, as I said, is is leaking.

167
00:11:18,270 --> 00:11:22,829
That's something else that we've also looked to address on the tail end of our research.

168
00:11:22,829 --> 00:11:25,790
And in general, just hallucinations.

169
00:11:25,790 --> 00:11:29,470
Models will come out with functions that do not exist.

170
00:11:29,470 --> 00:11:34,830
It will be sketching out some code that uses an upstream library, a dependency,

171
00:11:34,840 --> 00:11:36,630
and it will just make up names.

172
00:11:36,630 --> 00:11:39,830
It will come out with function names that don't exist.

173
00:11:39,830 --> 00:11:42,510
And there is a chance that if that's happening at a high frequency,

174
00:11:42,520 --> 00:11:50,790
then somebody could attempt to kind of squat on these hallucinated names and weaponize them.

175
00:11:50,790 --> 00:11:52,310
So, those are some of the key risks.

176
00:11:52,310 --> 00:11:57,270
And I mean, there are other things such as prompt injections and insecure models.

177
00:11:57,270 --> 00:11:59,030
Models have, you know, they use PyTorch and you've got.

178
00:11:59,040 --> 00:12:01,030
It's not used so much now, but pickle,

179
00:12:01,040 --> 00:12:06,550
which was susceptible to various attacks, and they started to use something called safe tensors,

180
00:12:06,550 --> 00:12:08,510
which is a lot more safer.

181
00:12:08,510 --> 00:12:14,470
now around the execution of code, but I would say those are the key aspects, the key security concerns.

182
00:12:14,480 --> 00:12:18,710
All right, quite a few key points over there,

183
00:12:18,710 --> 00:12:20,510
and we will double click on two of them specifically.

184
00:12:20,510 --> 00:12:24,630
One is secrets management, and the other one is dependency injection in this session.

185
00:12:24,640 --> 00:12:31,990
It's a good leeway into the next theme of the podcast, which is around secrets management.

186
00:12:31,990 --> 00:12:35,070
You've elaborated on what kind of secrets could be leaked,

187
00:12:35,070 --> 00:12:40,710
but what risk does the passage of secrets to LLMs pose?

188
00:12:40,710 --> 00:12:43,430
Is it primarily that once it's used in training, it could be exposed?

189
00:12:43,440 --> 00:12:49,990
Or are there other risks because of LLMs having that secrets within its context?

190
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:55,470
I guess it would be who receives the secret.

191
00:12:55,470 --> 00:12:59,910
Okay, so there are some organizations, corporations that we're going to feel a level of trust towards.

192
00:12:59,920 --> 00:13:03,990
If they're a large business that has a reputation,

193
00:13:03,990 --> 00:13:08,990
why would they want to use an individual's key to exploit a system? It's not very likely to happen.

194
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:13,150
Mhm. So, I would say with these large vendors that have these frontier models,

195
00:13:13,150 --> 00:13:21,151
the risk is much likely going to be that the large language model regurgitates a secret. or a key that it's discovered.

196
00:13:21,160 --> 00:13:29,990
Now, if you were to use a provider who is less well established, does not have a brand.

197
00:13:29,990 --> 00:13:32,110
that they're going to be very protective of,

198
00:13:32,110 --> 00:13:37,710
does not have a core business that's financially lucrative enough.

199
00:13:37,710 --> 00:13:40,230
that they don't need to resort to nefarious malicious actions, then.

200
00:13:40,230 --> 00:13:42,070
that could be a risk.

201
00:13:42,070 --> 00:13:44,230
I mean, this was one of the things that people,

202
00:13:44,230 --> 00:13:49,630
perhaps rightly or wrongly, inferred for Deep Seek when they put up an API.

203
00:13:49,630 --> 00:13:51,390
I have absolutely no opinion myself there.

204
00:13:51,400 --> 00:13:57,230
People were effectively saying, you know, your secrets are being leaked to a company.

205
00:13:57,230 --> 00:14:02,350
which operates outside of the jurisdiction of where most software engineers and companies tend to operate.

206
00:14:02,360 --> 00:14:06,070
So, I'm actually not insinuating anything at all there myself.

207
00:14:06,070 --> 00:14:11,150
The Deep Seek people seem like very bright, smart people,

208
00:14:11,150 --> 00:14:16,790
but there was this element of if these models are the inference points are much wider.

209
00:14:16,790 --> 00:14:19,070
and there are many more providers, then.

210
00:14:19,070 --> 00:14:23,350
obviously your secrets are going to be leaked to many more APIs, many more cloud services.

211
00:14:23,350 --> 00:14:27,550
They're going to be stored in many more logs and monitoring systems and so forth.

212
00:14:27,550 --> 00:14:33,829
And so, somebody effectively with bad intentions could possibly exfiltrate those secrets and use them.

213
00:14:33,839 --> 00:14:42,550
And how easy is it for malicious actors to extract secrets or secret data from LLMs?

214
00:14:42,550 --> 00:14:45,950
Is it through prompt engineering or are there other methods they use?

215
00:14:45,960 --> 00:14:48,710
It would be through prompt engineering.

216
00:14:48,720 --> 00:14:51,110
I would say it's not easy.

217
00:14:51,110 --> 00:14:58,871
It would require probably a prompt injection attack to get the large language model to break out of. its safeguards.

218
00:14:58,871 --> 00:15:04,670
So, large language models are trained so that they have a set of guardrails around.

219
00:15:04,670 --> 00:15:09,790
There's certain dialogue that they will not enter that consists of physical harm or hate and so forth.

220
00:15:09,790 --> 00:15:11,350
And security is another one of them.

221
00:15:11,350 --> 00:15:13,470
If you ask a lot of large language models,

222
00:15:13,470 --> 00:15:18,910
tell me how to write a Bitcoin miner or tell me how to write a script.

223
00:15:18,910 --> 00:15:21,270
that exploits somebody's computer, it will refuse.

224
00:15:21,270 --> 00:15:23,310
It has its guardrails.

225
00:15:23,310 --> 00:15:25,350
But, they can be broken out of this.

226
00:15:25,350 --> 00:15:30,350
There's a sequence of words that we'll use which will allow them to drop all of that context,

227
00:15:30,350 --> 00:15:32,870
that protection.

