450
00:31:05,670 --> 00:31:08,590
And then it would say, "And then run npm install bad package."

451
00:31:08,600 --> 00:31:12,230
And obviously replacing the bad package name here.

452
00:31:12,230 --> 00:31:17,550
And so it was effectively telling people, "Yep, go ahead, use this package.

453
00:31:17,550 --> 00:31:21,990
In fact, here's how to install the package." Which would have resulted in their machines being backdoored. So that was one of the reasons.

454
00:31:21,990 --> 00:31:29,510
that we started to look at introducing this system that could protect people against these malicious packages.

455
00:31:29,510 --> 00:31:33,790
Because it's was quite clear that we were asking some of the top coding assistants,

456
00:31:33,790 --> 00:31:38,230
some of the top models, "Could we use a bad package?"

457
00:31:38,230 --> 00:31:41,550
And it would say, "Certainly you can, and here's how to."

458
00:31:41,550 --> 00:31:44,510
So that was something that we saw quite commonly.

459
00:31:44,510 --> 00:31:47,830
Which was obviously a very, very clear security risk.

460
00:31:47,840 --> 00:31:55,870
And I believe the reason for this was the topic you mentioned about training data being stale.

461
00:31:55,870 --> 00:31:59,030
So LLM had not caught on to the fact that this was a malicious package.

462
00:31:59,040 --> 00:32:02,190
Yes, very much.

463
00:32:02,190 --> 00:32:04,230
And also, a lot of the time when these packages,

464
00:32:04,230 --> 00:32:10,550
these malicious packages are crafted, what they will do is they will choose a name.

465
00:32:10,560 --> 00:32:14,990
that is very close to the name of a popular package.

466
00:32:14,990 --> 00:32:19,990
So for example, there is the the Python package called requests,

467
00:32:19,990 --> 00:32:22,350
which a lot of people use as a HTTP client.

468
00:32:22,350 --> 00:32:25,511
It's very, very widely used. Millions of downloads a week.

469
00:32:25,511 --> 00:32:29,790
And you could take the S and you can maybe switch swap it to a Z,

470
00:32:29,790 --> 00:32:32,311
requests with a Z. Okay?

471
00:32:32,311 --> 00:32:34,190
That's called a typosquatting attack.

472
00:32:34,190 --> 00:32:36,270
The idea is somebody fumbles on a keyboard,

473
00:32:36,270 --> 00:32:39,910
they make a typo, they download and install your package and they're compromised.

474
00:32:39,910 --> 00:32:42,790
Now, the thing is with neural networks,

475
00:32:42,790 --> 00:32:48,150
they're a very, very large collection of vectorized weights and biases, okay?

476
00:32:48,150 --> 00:32:53,390
And how they work is they take natural language and they tokenize it.

477
00:32:53,390 --> 00:32:57,870
And then they perform these multiplication matrix multiplication,

478
00:32:57,870 --> 00:33:03,430
these various math takes place where they calculate the predicted next token.

479
00:33:03,430 --> 00:33:05,790
You know, there's all sorts of things such as a cosine distance,

480
00:33:05,790 --> 00:33:11,630
and they're effectively taking words, breaking them down into these mathematical numbers.

481
00:33:11,630 --> 00:33:16,590
and then they're looking for a close proximity patterns within the neural network. That's probably not the right language to use.

482
00:33:16,600 --> 00:33:21,550
If somebody there's a neural network expert, they'll probably be raising their eyebrows,

483
00:33:21,550 --> 00:33:23,550
but I'm just trying to make this sort of accessible for all.

484
00:33:23,560 --> 00:33:29,590
They find a very similar close proximity matches.

485
00:33:29,600 --> 00:33:35,310
And then they again decode that to natural language.

486
00:33:35,310 --> 00:33:40,910
So, if you make spelling mistakes with an LLM, it often won't mention them.

487
00:33:40,910 --> 00:33:42,630
It won't pull you up on them.

488
00:33:42,630 --> 00:33:44,550
because it knows what you're trying to say.

489
00:33:44,550 --> 00:33:49,311
because it knows of words that are of close proximity to the word. that you've used.

490
00:33:49,320 --> 00:33:55,670
And within the wider context, it's able to match similarity.

491
00:33:55,670 --> 00:34:00,590
It's able to sort of find similar calculations that produce a result of a natural language response.

492
00:34:00,600 --> 00:34:04,030
So, you might have the dog sat on the mat, okay?

493
00:34:04,030 --> 00:34:11,190
It's a very similar mathematically to a very sort of very close set of words.

494
00:34:11,190 --> 00:34:13,310
and letters when tokenized into a neural network.

495
00:34:13,310 --> 00:34:17,470
And so, you can start to see where if you would said to not a neural network.

496
00:34:17,470 --> 00:34:22,590
or to a large language model, "Uh how can I use package name with slight typo?"

497
00:34:22,600 --> 00:34:24,990
it's going to calculate.

498
00:34:24,990 --> 00:34:31,510
Calculate probably not being the right word that you are inferring this particular popular package.

499
00:34:31,510 --> 00:34:35,270
And cuz it's such a popular package, it would have appeared a lot within its training data.

500
00:34:35,270 --> 00:34:41,629
So, the weights and biases would naturally allow it to match to the popular package.

501
00:34:41,639 --> 00:34:44,110
Okay.

502
00:34:44,110 --> 00:34:49,389
So, that's a good example of where large language models are prone to not being aware of typosquatting. attacks, you know.

503
00:34:49,399 --> 00:34:52,510
There's that one that they do quite often.

504
00:34:52,510 --> 00:34:55,070
They say, "How many Rs in strawberry?"

505
00:34:55,070 --> 00:34:57,270
And the LLM is just convinced that it's two.

506
00:34:57,270 --> 00:35:01,710
And you try to convince it it's three, and it just it will not have it.

507
00:35:01,710 --> 00:35:03,670
It's you know, it's to do with this uh vectorization.

508
00:35:03,670 --> 00:35:09,790
They're these huge high-dimensional spaces of these float numbers effectively.

509
00:35:09,790 --> 00:35:15,430
These these vectors and they calculate similar distances around words.

510
00:35:15,430 --> 00:35:17,390
That's how they appear to be human.

511
00:35:17,390 --> 00:35:19,790
So, there's that that's obviously a key risk as well.

512
00:35:19,800 --> 00:35:24,590
On the example that you gave for typosquatting for requests package,

513
00:35:24,600 --> 00:35:29,030
won't the LLM naturally pick up the package which is most popular?

514
00:35:29,030 --> 00:35:34,310
Why would it go and pick a package with a typo or a spelling mistake in it?

515
00:35:34,320 --> 00:35:37,030
Well, interestingly, they do.

516
00:35:37,030 --> 00:35:39,510
That's the interesting thing.

517
00:35:39,510 --> 00:35:44,550
When you do give them a word which is has a slight character difference,

518
00:35:44,550 --> 00:35:46,350
they still will go with that recommendation.

519
00:35:46,350 --> 00:35:53,310
Go with that recommendation is probably not the right way to articulate speaking about a neural network,

520
00:35:53,310 --> 00:35:56,951
but they do go along with the original word that you gave them. I'm not sure why that is.

521
00:35:56,951 --> 00:36:00,830
I mean, you know, these these neural networks, there's a whole study group.

522
00:36:00,830 --> 00:36:04,350
that are trying to figure out what's going on inside them really cuz you know,

523
00:36:04,350 --> 00:36:05,950
there are aspects of them.

524
00:36:05,950 --> 00:36:09,310
that we still don't understand really how they operate certain way,

525
00:36:09,310 --> 00:36:10,950
but it's just a pattern that they they do follow.

526
00:36:10,960 --> 00:36:21,711
Do the security risks differ between companies using proprietary models like GitHub Copilot versus open-source models like Deep. Seek or StarCoder?

527
00:36:21,720 --> 00:36:25,030
Well, good question.

528
00:36:25,030 --> 00:36:29,030
Now, I think we could kind of bring this up a few notches,

529
00:36:29,030 --> 00:36:32,790
where to trust a model, you need to peel back the layers effectively.

530
00:36:32,790 --> 00:36:37,950
So, this is about model provenance, understanding the source of origin of a model.

531
00:36:37,950 --> 00:36:40,110
Okay, so you start with the training data.

532
00:36:40,110 --> 00:36:46,070
Okay, and if there are any risks that are introduced, it's going to be at that stage.

533
00:36:46,070 --> 00:36:49,430
And for the closed-source models, you have no idea of the training data that they use.

534
00:36:49,430 --> 00:36:51,590
You have no idea of the weights.

535
00:36:51,590 --> 00:36:53,950
Okay, so there's very little transparency.

536
00:36:53,950 --> 00:36:56,110
If you make any sort of trust,

537
00:36:56,110 --> 00:37:00,110
it's based on them being an established corporation with a brand to protect, like I spoke about earlier.

538
00:37:00,120 --> 00:37:06,750
Now, open-source models, there are I mean, people call open-source models open-source models,

539
00:37:06,750 --> 00:37:09,870
but there is some nuance to this effectively.

540
00:37:09,870 --> 00:37:12,470
Some models are they have an open license,

541
00:37:12,470 --> 00:37:19,110
so they have an unrestricted license, like Llama, for example, which deems people to label them as open-source.

542
00:37:19,110 --> 00:37:23,150
but they're not really truly open-source in a way,

543
00:37:23,150 --> 00:37:26,590
because the weights are not open or the the data.

544
00:37:26,590 --> 00:37:28,910
that was used to train the model is is not freely available.

545
00:37:28,920 --> 00:37:33,310
So, for you to be able to really trust the model, whether it's open.

546
00:37:33,310 --> 00:37:37,590
or closed, I guess by definition of it being closed,

547
00:37:37,590 --> 00:37:39,670
this would not be possible, you would need to have reproducibility.

548
00:37:39,680 --> 00:37:44,790
You'd need to be able to take the data set, train the model,

549
00:37:44,790 --> 00:37:46,390
and then end up with an identical model.

550
00:37:46,390 --> 00:37:51,071
It's a reproducible training flow. The pipeline is reproducible.

551
00:37:51,071 --> 00:37:55,710
And that is really the only way to be able to 100% trust a model is to have.

552
00:37:55,710 --> 00:38:00,390
that level of provenance, to be able to take all the component parts.

553
00:38:00,390 --> 00:38:03,030
and end up with the same result in large language model.

554
00:38:03,040 --> 00:38:10,470
So, I guess, can you trust open-source models with there being different variations of open-source models?

555
00:38:10,480 --> 00:38:15,350
Perhaps, because there is a bit more transparency.

556
00:38:15,350 --> 00:38:17,590
That is certainly a a factor, but to truly trust the model,

557
00:38:17,600 --> 00:38:20,910
you would need to have the actual training data, as well.

558
00:38:20,910 --> 00:38:24,270
You would need and you would need the computational resources to perform the training,

559
00:38:24,280 --> 00:38:29,750
which, let's be honest, very few of us have.

560
00:38:29,750 --> 00:38:34,110
Now, one of the efforts that is looking to solve this is part of the Six Store project,

561
00:38:34,110 --> 00:38:36,270
which I started a few years ago.

562
00:38:36,270 --> 00:38:41,950
And there is some work around model provenance, which is just kind of 1.0 release.

563
00:38:41,950 --> 00:38:44,430
And this is being used by various people such as Google.

564
00:38:44,430 --> 00:38:51,430
and Nvidia and and other entities are starting to either explore or leverage this form of provenance.

565
00:38:51,440 --> 00:38:59,990
And this consists of cryptographic signing of the different flows of a training pipeline.

566
00:38:59,990 --> 00:39:04,790
So, that you can then get guarantees around the integrity and the provenance of the model.

567
00:39:04,790 --> 00:39:06,550
So, there is some good work going in.

568
00:39:06,550 --> 00:39:11,190
that direction to make these models more accountable and transparent and.

569
00:39:11,190 --> 00:39:14,830
and for you to have some guarantees around the source of origin,

570
00:39:14,830 --> 00:39:17,510
like who did actually train this model?

571
00:39:17,510 --> 00:39:20,950
Is it the person or the people that it claims to originate from?

572
00:39:20,950 --> 00:39:25,510
With a cryptographic provenance, you can get those assurances.

573
00:39:25,510 --> 00:39:30,270
that it is a model that comes from the entity that it claims to have come from.

574
00:39:30,270 --> 00:39:35,150
So, again, this is same problem, same technology that's just rehashed for a new technology.

575
00:39:35,150 --> 00:39:39,710
We we had this for quite a while with container images and packages,

576
00:39:39,710 --> 00:39:44,110
understanding the source of origin, supply chain security, an area that I've I've I've worked in.

577
00:39:44,120 --> 00:39:46,030
for quite a while myself.

578
00:39:46,030 --> 00:39:50,671
All of these technologies have been repurposed for the AI age. So, it's.

579
00:39:50,671 --> 00:39:53,470
It's like any, you know, the old ideas to the new ideas.

580
00:39:53,480 --> 00:39:57,830
All right, and make sure we link to Six Store in our podcast notes.

581
00:39:57,840 --> 00:40:02,750
Sure. Before we wrap up, do you have any thoughts on what's next in AI security?

582
00:40:02,760 --> 00:40:08,150
What are the emerging risks and what are the key mitigating solutions that are being worked on?

583
00:40:08,160 --> 00:40:10,030
That's a very good question.

584
00:40:10,030 --> 00:40:12,110
It's a very good question.

585
00:40:12,110 --> 00:40:14,190
I think a lot of what I've spoken about today are.

586
00:40:14,190 --> 00:40:17,390
yet to really sort of escalate as being problems.

587
00:40:17,390 --> 00:40:23,270
So, I think a lot of what we've touched upon today are some of the future risks.

588
00:40:23,270 --> 00:40:27,310
But, outside of that, I think, you know, the things that are particularly concerning,

589
00:40:27,320 --> 00:40:29,590
and this perhaps goes above security as.

590
00:40:29,600 --> 00:40:33,990
well, is how influential these things are becoming in our lives, okay?

591
00:40:34,000 --> 00:40:46,510
Personal counselors, accountants, they're becoming deeply ingrained in our lives, large language models, and they appear very trustworthy.

592
00:40:46,510 --> 00:40:49,231
The guide rails are working well. Our models seem safe, okay?

593
00:40:49,231 --> 00:40:56,631
But, these models can experience pretty dramatic malfunctions. They really can.

594
00:40:56,640 --> 00:40:59,390
Catastrophic forgetting, overfitting.

595
00:40:59,400 --> 00:41:06,110
These are things that we've seen play out with frontier widely available models,

596
00:41:06,110 --> 00:41:10,390
but there is that intrinsic capacity for a model to really malfunction on a level.

597
00:41:10,390 --> 00:41:16,950
that could completely cause mayhem if other systems are relying on that model for,

598
00:41:16,950 --> 00:41:21,550
you know, automation of infrastructure and the services.

599
00:41:21,550 --> 00:41:23,990
that we rely on to be present every day for society to function.

600
00:41:23,990 --> 00:41:29,750
As LLMs start to become more of a guiding factor in our lives,

601
00:41:29,750 --> 00:41:32,470
the safety of LLMs becomes absolutely critical.

602
00:41:32,470 --> 00:41:39,510
And we we spent some time tuning large language models, trying to tune them to be more security-aware,

603
00:41:39,510 --> 00:41:41,630
and we experienced them go bad robot, and you know.

604
00:41:41,630 --> 00:41:45,030
that it really doesn't take much. You overfit them to a certain degree,

605
00:41:45,030 --> 00:41:51,150
and then suddenly they are just spitting out the same word repeatedly over. and over and over and over.

606
00:41:51,150 --> 00:41:53,151
You're just trying to kill the process to stop it. It's.

607
00:41:53,151 --> 00:41:55,710
It's effectively gone fully to defectual.

608
00:41:55,710 --> 00:41:58,750
So, I think that is a real concern.

609
00:41:58,750 --> 00:42:01,110
AI is creeping more and more into our lives.

610
00:42:01,120 --> 00:42:02,670
You know, we're starting to rely on it.

